import{_ as o,o as a,c as t,a3 as c}from"./chunks/framework.DAK02U1-.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"macOS","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"macOS | clice.io"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://docs.clice.io/xclang/design/macos"}],["meta",{"property":"og:image","content":"https://docs.clice.io/og.png"}],["meta",{"property":"og:image:width","content":"1200"}],["meta",{"property":"og:image:height","content":"630"}],["meta",{"property":"og:locale","content":"en_US"}],["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://docs.clice.io/xclang/design/macos"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://docs.clice.io/xclang/design/macos"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://docs.clice.io/xclang/design/macos"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://docs.clice.io/zh/xclang/design/macos"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"xclang/design/macos.md","filePath":"en/xclang/design/macos.md","lastUpdated":1794509097000}'),i={name:"xclang/design/macos.md"};function s(d,e,n,r,l,h){return a(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[c('<h1 id="macos" tabindex="-1">macOS <a class="header-anchor" href="#macos" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;macOS&quot;">​</a></h1><p>How xclang&#39;s macOS targets, <code>xclang sdk fetch macos</code>, <code>ld64.lld</code> and the macOS host toolchains are built and configured. How to compile for macOS targets is in <a href="./../guide/cross-compiling#macos">cross-compiling</a>.</p><h2 id="why-xclang-s-libc-and-not-the-system-s" tabindex="-1">Why xclang&#39;s <code>libc++</code> and Not the System&#39;s <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-xclang-s-libc-and-not-the-system-s" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why xclang&#39;s `libc++` and Not the System&#39;s&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The macOS target config files put xclang&#39;s <code>libc++</code> headers (<code>-stdlib++-isystem</code>) on the C++ include path and place xclang&#39;s static <code>libc++.a</code> (which includes <code>libc++abi</code>) ahead of the SDK&#39;s <code>libc++.tbd</code>. The unwinder is the system&#39;s, in <code>libSystem</code> (<a href="./hermeticity">hermeticity</a>).</p><p>Linking xclang&#39;s <code>libc++</code> statically has two advantages:</p><ul><li><strong>OS-independent C++ library features</strong>: Apple&#39;s SDK marks newer <code>libc++</code> features with availability attributes tied to the macOS version whose <code>/usr/lib/libc++.1.dylib</code> first shipped them. With xclang&#39;s <code>libc++</code> linked into the binary, standard library features depend on the xclang release rather than the target macOS version (for example, <code>std::format</code> of a floating-point number works at <code>-mmacos-version-min=13.0</code>, whereas with the system&#39;s <code>libc++</code> it requires <code>13.3</code> or later).</li><li><strong>Same <code>libc++</code> across platforms</strong>: programs get the same <code>libc++</code> implementation on macOS, Linux and Windows.</li></ul><p>Passing <code>-stdlib=platform</code> switches to <code>bin/&lt;triple&gt;-system.cfg</code> (<a href="./../reference/patches">patch 0018</a>), which keeps the SDK, deployment target and <code>ld64.lld</code> and links <code>/usr/lib/libc++.1.dylib</code> of the OS (<a href="./../guide/cross-compiling#the-platform-s-c-library">cross-compiling</a>).</p><p>The <code>x86_64-apple-darwin</code> host toolchain itself is cross-compiled on <code>arm64</code> macOS, with its table generators built for <code>arm64</code> first so that no <code>x86_64</code> program runs under Rosetta during the build, and tested on an <code>x86_64</code> Mac (<code>macos-15-intel</code>).</p><h2 id="apple-s-sdk-without-xcode" tabindex="-1">Apple&#39;s SDK without Xcode <a class="header-anchor" href="#apple-s-sdk-without-xcode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Apple&#39;s SDK without Xcode&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Apple&#39;s macOS SDK is distributed inside the Command Line Tools <code>.pkg</code> archive on <code>swcdn.apple.com</code>, listed in Apple&#39;s software update catalogs (<code>swscan.apple.com</code>) without an Apple ID. The <code>.pkg</code> is a <code>xar</code> archive whose <code>Payload</code> is a <code>pbzx</code> stream of <code>xz</code> chunks containing a <code>cpio</code> archive. <code>xclang</code> reads <code>xar</code>, <code>pbzx</code>, <code>xz</code> and <code>cpio</code> with its own code, so <code>xclang sdk fetch macos</code> works on Linux, Windows and macOS without macOS tools (<a href="./../reference/xclang-command">the <code>xclang</code> command</a>).</p><p>During extraction, <code>xclang</code> unpacks <code>Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/SDKs/MacOSX&lt;ver&gt;.sdk</code> and leaves out <code>usr/bin/</code>, <code>usr/share/</code> (whose man page names contain <code>:</code> characters that Windows file systems reject) and <code>System/Library/Perl/</code>. On Windows, symbolic links in the SDK are created as directory junctions and file hard links by default (<code>--links junction</code>), which require no elevated privileges.</p><p>Unlike the <a href="./windows#config-files-and-object-directives">Windows MSVC targets</a>, which need generated config files to pass MSVC and Windows SDK version subdirectories, a macOS SDK needs only its root directory. Putting <code>-isysroot &lt;CFGDIR&gt;/../sdk/macos</code> directly in the Linux and Windows target config files allows an explicit <code>-isysroot</code> on the command line to work even when no SDK has been fetched into the toolchain.</p><h2 id="packages-apple-withdraws-and-content-digests" tabindex="-1">Packages Apple Withdraws and Content Digests <a class="header-anchor" href="#packages-apple-withdraws-and-content-digests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Packages Apple Withdraws and Content Digests&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Apple&#39;s software update catalogs list the Command Line Tools packages current for each macOS version. Periodically Apple withdraws a package (returning <code>403</code> or <code>404</code> on <code>swcdn.apple.com</code>) and posts a re-signed package in its place whose outer archive bytes differ while the SDK inside is unchanged: when product <code>142-50405</code> replaced <code>082-83364</code>, all 18,516 files of its macOS <code>26.5</code> SDK were identical.</p><p>To survive withdrawn packages without sacrificing reproducibility, <code>xclang/sdk-versions.json</code> pins each macOS package twice: by the <code>.pkg</code> archive&#39;s <code>sha256</code> and by the content digest of the SDK inside it. When Apple&#39;s CDN returns <code>403</code> or <code>404</code> for the pinned URL, <code>xclang sdk fetch</code> (and the <a href="./../integrations/bazel">Bazel</a> module&#39;s fallback) reads Apple&#39;s catalogs, inspects the start of candidate packages&#39; <code>Payload</code> to find the requested SDK version, and accepts the package once its unpacked SDK matches the pinned content digest.</p><p>The content digest is the <code>sha256</code> of the SDK&#39;s manifest, which has one line per regular file and symbolic link under <code>Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/SDKs/MacOSX&lt;ver&gt;.sdk/</code> in the package&#39;s <code>cpio</code> payload (including the man pages, tools and Perl files that <code>xclang</code> leaves out on disk), sorted byte-by-byte by path:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>file &lt;sha256 of its bytes&gt; &lt;path&gt;</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>link &lt;sha256 of its target&gt; &lt;path&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Each line ends with a newline, <code>&lt;path&gt;</code> is relative to the SDK directory with <code>/</code> separators, and a hard link is hashed as a <code>file</code> with its bytes. Directories, file modes, modification times and how <code>xclang</code> creates links on Windows do not affect the digest; <code>pkgutil --expand-full</code> on a Mac unpacks the same tree.</p><p>Four consequences follow from this pinning model:</p><ul><li><strong>A version Apple no longer carries fails</strong>: when Apple replaces an SDK version in its catalogs (for example, when <code>27.2</code> replaced <code>27.0</code>), no catalog package carries the old version, and fetching it fails with a list of the versions Apple&#39;s catalogs currently offer.</li><li><strong>One version string is not one SDK</strong>: earlier Command Line Tools builds of SDK <code>26.5</code> differed from the pinned <code>26.5</code> in 9 files; a package for the same version string whose SDK content differs from the pinned digest is refused.</li><li><strong><code>--version latest</code> is not reproducible</strong>: it takes whatever newest SDK Apple&#39;s catalogs list at the time it runs, which may be newer than the version table and unpinned.</li><li><strong>xclang keeps no mirror</strong>: xclang never caches or redistributes Apple&#39;s packages on its own servers. A <code>.pkg</code> you save in the <code>--cache</code> directory of <code>xclang sdk fetch</code>, or in Bazel&#39;s repository cache or <code>--distdir</code> (<a href="./../integrations/ci">CI</a>), continues to build after Apple removes that version from its CDN.</li></ul><h2 id="why-ld64-lld-and-patch-0007" tabindex="-1">Why <code>ld64.lld</code> and Patch 0007 <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-ld64-lld-and-patch-0007" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why `ld64.lld` and Patch 0007&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Using <code>ld64.lld</code> on macOS as well as Linux and Windows gives every host the same Mach-O linker, signs <code>arm64</code> binaries ad hoc without invoking <code>codesign</code>, supports LTO with the compiler&#39;s own LLVM version without shipping a 120 MB <code>libLTO.dylib</code> or passing <code>-lto_library</code>, and accepts the same flags on every host for the ThinLTO cache (<code>-cache_path_lto</code>) and relative debug maps (<code>-oso_prefix</code>).</p><p>When <code>clang</code> compiles and links in a single command with ThinLTO, it passes <code>-object_path_lto</code> to the linker. With <code>-object_path_lto</code>, the LTO backend writes an empty object file first whose only symbol, <code>ltmp0</code>, has the same address as the function that follows it (<code>main</code>). Upstream <code>ld64.lld</code> collected unwind entries from every symbol in code sections, so the empty unwind entry of <code>ltmp0</code> overwrote <code>main</code>&#39;s entry in <code>__unwind_info</code> on <code>arm64</code>, preventing exceptions from unwinding through <code>main</code> (<code>llvm/llvm-project#224949</code>, fixed by <code>llvm/llvm-project#225055</code>). <a href="./../reference/patches">Patch 0007</a> carries that fix in xclang.</p><p>Xcode 27&#39;s macOS 27 SDK also introduced a new architecture name, <code>arm64e.x1</code>, in the <code>.tbd</code> stubs of <code>libSystem</code> and the system frameworks, which LLVM&#39;s TextAPI in xclang supports.</p><h2 id="debug-information-on-mach-o" tabindex="-1">Debug Information on Mach-O <a class="header-anchor" href="#debug-information-on-mach-o" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Debug Information on Mach-O&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Mach-O links keep DWARF debug information in the <code>.o</code> object files and record their paths in the executable&#39;s debug map (<code>N_OSO</code> stabs), from which <code>dsymutil</code> collects a <code>.dSYM</code> bundle (<a href="./../features/debugging">debugging</a>):</p><ul><li><strong>Relative paths in the debug map</strong>: by default, the debug map records each object file by its absolute path, so linking the same program in two directories produces different bytes. Bazel links pass <code>-oso_prefix .</code> so the debug map records relative <code>bazel-out/...</code> paths.</li><li><strong>ThinLTO temporary objects</strong>: with ThinLTO, the LTO backend writes temporary object files that the linker deletes when the link finishes, so xclang&#39;s <a href="./../integrations/cmake">CMake</a> and <a href="./../integrations/bazel">Bazel</a> integrations pass <code>-object_path_lto</code> and generate the <code>.dSYM</code> bundle during the link step (<a href="./../features/debugging#why-a-dsym-comes-from-the-link">why a dSYM comes from the link</a>).</li><li><strong>Stripping</strong>: release binaries strip with <code>llvm-strip --strip-all</code> (matching Apple&#39;s <code>strip</code>), and optimized Bazel builds pass <code>-Wl,-no_exported_symbols</code> so stripped executables retain no exported weak C++ symbols that <code>dladdr</code> would misattribute crash frames to (<a href="./../features/debugging#strip">strip</a>).</li></ul><h2 id="the-sanitizers-dylibs" tabindex="-1">The Sanitizers&#39; dylibs <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-sanitizers-dylibs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Sanitizers&#39; dylibs&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Both macOS targets support AddressSanitizer (with the ASan <code>libc++</code>), ThreadSanitizer, LeakSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and libFuzzer. Their runtimes are universal <code>.dylib</code> files (<code>libclang_rt.*_osx_dynamic.dylib</code> in <code>lib/clang/&lt;major&gt;/lib/darwin/</code>) loaded at run time from the toolchain or the binary&#39;s directory, as a test-only exception to <a href="./hermeticity">hermeticity</a> (<a href="./../features/sanitizers">sanitizers</a>). In xclang&#39;s toolchain build, <code>compiler-rt</code> for Darwin is linked with <code>ZERO_AR_DATE=1</code> so object archive timestamps do not enter the <code>.dylib</code> debug map or Mach-O UUID.</p>',29)])])}const m=o(i,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
